[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Asynchronous Programming in Flutter: Futures, Streams, and async/await"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Asynchronous programming is a core concept in Flutter development. Because Flutter apps run on a single-threaded execution model (the Dart isolate), any long-running operations like network requests, database queries, or file I/O must be handled asynchronously to prevent the user interface from freezing."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Futures"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A Future represents a computation that will complete at some point in the future. It is analogous to a promise in JavaScript. A Future can be in one of two states: uncompleted or completed (either with a value or with an error)."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"Future<String> fetchUserData() {
  return Future.delayed(Duration(seconds: 2), () => "User: John Doe");
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Using async and await"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"The async and await keywords provide a declarative way to write asynchronous code that reads like synchronous code. When you mark a function with async, you can use the await keyword to pause execution until the Future resolves."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"void loadData() async {
  print("Loading...");
  String data = await fetchUserData();
  print(data);
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Streams"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"While a Future handles a single asynchronous value, a Stream provides a sequence of asynchronous events. Streams are ideal for representing real-time data sources, such as chat messages, sensor readings, or Firebase Firestore snapshots."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"Stream<int> countStream(int max) async* {
  for (int i = 1; i <= max; i++) {
    await Future.delayed(Duration(seconds: 1));
    yield i;
  }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Consuming Streams with StreamBuilder"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"In Flutter, the StreamBuilder widget is the most efficient way to rebuild your UI dynamically in response to incoming stream data. It listens to the stream and automatically triggers a rebuild whenever a new event arrives."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"StreamBuilder<int>(
  stream: countStream(5),
  builder: (context, snapshot) {
    if (snapshot.connectionState == ConnectionState.waiting) {
      return CircularProgressIndicator();
    } else if (snapshot.hasError) {
      return Text("Error: \${snapshot.error}");
    } else {
      return Text("Count: \${snapshot.data}");
    }
  },
)"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always handle errors in Futures using try-catch blocks or the .catchError() method."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Remember to cancel your Stream subscriptions when they are no longer needed to prevent memory leaks."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Keep your UI responsive by offloading heavy computations to separate isolates using compute()."}]
